# Site configurations

Every site the user hands over gets an entry in `sites/sites.json`, shown read-only in the app's **Sites** panel. The app matches a queued URL's host against `match` (domain or subdomain) and runs the entry's `recipe`; `sites/plugins` is always passed with `--plugin-dirs`. There are no raw per-site yt-dlp options: anything a site needs is a recipe field.

The goal is no site-specific settings at all: every site should work through built-in recipes the learner finds on its own, so entries are records of what was tested, not configuration that needs maintaining and shipping.

When given a URL, establish success yourself:

1. Probe without downloading: `curl -I`, then `yt-dlp --simulate` (full network permission).
2. Prove it with real media: download the file or a sample into a scratch folder under `/tmp` (never `~/Downloads`), check it with `ffprobe` and a full `ffmpeg -f null` decode, then delete it.
3. If it fails, fix in this order: update yt-dlp, an existing built-in recipe, a new general capability or built-in in the recipe engine, the user's browser session (ask first; recorded as `"session": true`), and only if no recipe can express it, a plugin in `sites/plugins/siphon/yt_dlp_plugins/extractor/` (yt-dlp ignores a `yt_dlp_plugins` folder placed directly in the plugin dir). The entry's `plugin` path is relative to `sites/plugins`.
   - Built-ins first: a site recipe is a bare built-in (`"base": "followEmbed" | "embedPage" | "smartScan" | "playerConfig" | "pageSources" | "playerAPI"`, defined in `Recipe.swift`). If no built-in fits, find the general technique (a player-software convention, a platform's data address, an address form a yt-dlp extractor knows) and add it to the engine or as a new built-in plus a learner attempt, rather than writing site-specific fields. Check that the learner's order finds the site with no entry, using real sample downloads.
   - A recipe with hand-written fields beyond its built-in is a last resort; the learner never replaces it automatically (it only reports failures), so it needs maintaining by hand. Tell the user when a site can't be done generally and let them decide.
   - Keep plugins for real code (computed signatures, multi-step APIs). Retired plugins go to `sites/plugins-archive/` with restore steps in its README.
   - A `recipe` is a JSON object the native app (`mac/Siphon/Engine/Recipe.swift`) runs before yt-dlp. It only fetches and reads text; nothing from the site runs. Fields:
     - `download`: how yt-dlp itself downloads: `impersonate` (pose as desktop Chrome), `siteReferer` (the site's home page as referer), `scanPage` (skip yt-dlp's own support and scan the page). A recipe with only `download` gives yt-dlp the page itself (`media` defaults to `page`).
     - `session`: the site needs the user's browser sign-in. Recorded only so far; Siphon must ask before ever using cookies.
     - `fetch`: `auto` (default: plain request, falling back to `BrowserFetch`, yt-dlp impersonating desktop Chrome, on a 403/503 or Cloudflare challenge) or `browser` (always).
     - `embed`: follow the player iframe. `match` (regex for its src, or omit for the most player-like iframe), `referer` (fetch it with the page as referer, default true), `watchPage` (give yt-dlp the player's own video page: `/embed-ID` → `/video-ID/`, `/embed/ID` → `/video/ID/`, without a referer).
     - `link`: instead of an iframe, a URL built from the page: `find` (regex with one group, tried on the page URL then the page) fills `$1` in `url`. Fetched with the page as referer and an Accept header that includes JSON, for player data addresses; a top-level JSON `title` there becomes the title. With `media: ytdlp` the URL goes to yt-dlp without a referer.
     - `unescape`: undo `\"` `\/` `\u0026` JavaScript escaping.
     - `media`: `page`; `ytdlp` (give yt-dlp the player URL); `sources` (read `<source>`/`<video src>`, JSON source lists `{"src"|"file"|"url"|"link": …, "label": "720p"}` and files listed by quality name `{"lq"|"hq"|"4k"|"720p": url}`); `pattern` (the URL captured by `pattern`/`patterns`; KVS players' labelled files, `video_url` with `video_url_text`, across all player blocks, come first). Picks the tallest that fits the Quality setting, H.264 over AV1/HEVC at equal height, an unmeasured `HQ`/`High Quality` file over measured ones, and skips a pick whose server answers with a web page.
     - `title` (regex with one group, applied to the page), `mediaReferer`.
4. Record the entry: `method` (`builtin` | `recipe` | `plugin` | `unsupported`; older `"method": "embed"` / `"followEmbed": true` entries are converted to the followEmbed recipe when the app loads them), `status` (`working` | `broken` | `unsupported` | `untested`), `lastTested`, `testResult` (what the check showed), `notes` (why it failed and why the fix works), `exampleUrl`.
5. Do not circumvent DRM; mark such sites `unsupported` and say why. Don't work around yt-dlp's piracy refusals or reach members-only files.

## The app's persistent copy

- The native app keeps its own copy at `~/Library/Application Support/Siphon/sites/` (`sites.json` + `plugins/`), seeded from the bundled repo copy and merged on every launch: a repo entry replaces the entries it overlaps unless one of them has a later `lastTested`.
- Every download is also a test. For a site with no entry, or an entry the learner could have produced itself (no recipe, a bare built-in, or a learned entry), the app tries the saved recipe first, then its automatic fixes in order (plain yt-dlp, impersonation, impersonation with the site as referer, page scan, followEmbed, embedPage, smartScan, playerConfig, pageSources, playerAPI), checks the file with `ffprobe` (a video shorter than 8 s counts as a preview clip, not the video), and records the result with `"source": "learned"`. A recipe's page fetch retries a 404 with the site's home page as referer (some sites hide pages from requests without one). A 404/410 stops the learner as a removed video only when the video's own page answers 404/410 with and without that referer; a dead file or player behind a page that still loads means that fix doesn't fit, so the next one is tried. A broken entry keeps its recipe, which is still tried first next time. Unsupported entries and hand-written recipes are never replaced.
- When a built-in recipe works, the learner records it with what the download showed filled in (`SiteLearner.specialized`): the player iframe's host as `embed.match`, the player-config key that held the URL as `pattern`, and a title trim checked on the page (from `og:site_name` or the domain) as `title`. Stale details fall back to the plain built-ins and are re-recorded. `fetch: browser` is deliberately not learned (the default `auto` is faster when plain requests usually pass).
- The Sites window points out player hosts shared by two or more working sites; those are candidates for a new built-in, which still needs writing and checking by hand.
- Learned `broken` entries are the work list: fix them with the steps above, then record the fix in the repo's `sites/sites.json` with a newer `lastTested` so it wins the merge.
